-- title:
--   Weakly Cauchy free-space sequences are uniformly approximable on a ball
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a pointed metric space with Lipschitz-free space $\mathcal F(M)$; $\mathcal F_K(M)$ is the closed span of $\{\delta_x:x\in K\}$, and $\langle\mu,f\rangle$ is the pairing with a Lipschitz $f$ normalized by $f(0)=0$. An element $\mu$ is $\varepsilon$-approximable on $K$ if $\|\mu-\nu\|<\varepsilon$ for some $\nu\in\mathcal F_K(M)$. If $\mu_i$ is weakly Cauchy in $\mathcal F(M)$, then for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $R>0$ such that
--   $$
--   \mu_i\ \text{is }\varepsilon\text{-approximable on }\bar B(0,R)\quad\text{for all }i .
--   $$
--
--   This extends the radial reduction from weakly null to weakly Cauchy sequences.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/LipschitzEquivalence/CauchyApproximation.lean#L20-L66

